Voting factors:
- Status quo bias
- Your ideological beliefs
- Parents, family, friends
- Campaigns
- Media - debates, opinion pieces, etc
- Manifestos - policies, etc
- Party leader
- Local candidates
- Tactical voting - FPTP
- Social class, Background, Ethnicity, age, personal characteristics

*****************************************************************************************************

AGE:-

- Generally, support for the Conservatives. Even in 1997 and 2001, the Conservatives beat Labour in votes among the over 65s. Labour & the Green Party tend to do better with younger people, although they share this age group with the Lib Dems (evidenced in defections to the Lib Dems in 2005).

- Why? **People become more conservative with age**, perhaps because of increased wealth or increased fear of change:-

- Perhaps younger generations are more idealistic, wanting peace and justice for all, favouring policies of shared wealth through public spending and the tackling of global poverty – _hence perhaps 62% of 18–24-year-olds voting Labour in 2017 during Jeremy Corbyn’s ‘For the many not the few’ campaigns, versus 27% who voted Conservative_. _Likewise, 62% of 18–24-year-olds voted Labour in 2019, when Labour promised to abolish university tuition fees._
 - As they get older, they start paying more tax and they become cynical about the prospect of saving the world. _In 2019, people who owned their own homes voted Conservative by a margin of 35% over Labour, whilst 15% more private renters voted Labour than Conservative. Likewise, in 2019, despite winning the election, the Conservatives lost votes among young people – only 19% of 18–24-year-olds voted Conservative._
 - Both Labour and the Conservatives do best when they reach beyond their core vote – with Margaret Thatcher beating Labour by 1% in the 18–24-year-old category in 1979 (helping her win a landslide) and Tony Blair beating the Conservatives by 5% in 65+ year-old voters in 1997, leading to a Labour landslide.

*****************************************************************************************************

TURNOUT:

- Importantly, however, young people are also _less_ likely to turn out to vote than old people, too (e.g. 2017: 54% of 18–24-year-olds turned out (the lowest age group) whilst 85% of the 70+ age group turned out). _**So, the parties of the left tend to lose out when turnout is low.**_ Social media and online polling also tend to focus on the young, and because of their lower turnout, this can lead to surprises on polling day when Labour do worse than expected and the Conservatives do better.
- **The issue of the ‘grey vote’ – the support of older generations – will become more significant with Britain’s ageing population.** Because the young are less likely to actually vote, party policy tends to focus on issues that older people care about (NHS, pensions, law and order) and less on issues that younger people care about (university fees, youth unemployment, reform of drug laws).

*****************************************************************************************************

GENDER:-

- **Traditionally,** women in general voted Conservative whilst men voted Labour. Possible reasons for this relate to **roles in society**: unionised working men are likely to vote with their class-party, whilst stay-at-home women looking after the family are more in tune with Conservative values of law, order and the family. When Ted Heath (Cons) won the election in 1970, it was thought the ‘swing vote’ had come from housewives attracted to his policies of economic stability and protection for the price of a weekly shop.
- **In more recent times, the balance has shifted: in 1997, with the advent of Tony Blair and New Labour, equal numbers of men and women voted Labour. This trend of women towards Labour continued in 2001 and 2005. In 2001 women favoured Labour over Conservatives (38%:32%) whereas male support was evenly split between the parties (34%:34%).**

- Possible reasons include:
    - the changing roles of women in society (more women working)
    - the declining influence of unionised labour
    - or Labour’s “women-friendly” policies (inc. the creation of a Minister for Women; emphasis on 	 education, social care and the NHS).

- In 2017, the Conservatives won 6% more male votes than Labour, perhaps because men are more likely to prioritise issues like nuclear power, nuclear weapons & foreign intervention, which Theresa May emphasised. 2019: Conservatives = male 46%, female 43%; Labour = male 31%, female 34%; Lib Dems = male = 12%, female = 12%. Of course, this overlaps with age, e.g. 2019, focusing on just 18–24-year-old _women_ changes the stats to Conservatives = 17%, Labour = 64%, as opposed to males 18–24-year-olds voting Conservative 22% and Labour 59%.

*****************************************************************************************************

Ethnicity:-

- In 1996, the ethnic minority populations of Britain were around 3.3 million (6% of the population); by 2001, it was 4.6 million (8%); by 2014 it was 8 million (14%). Generally speaking, turnout among ethnic minorities is low (2015 BME turnout = 56% versus White turnout = 68%), and they tend to vote to the left of the political spectrum (2015: 65% BME vote Labour).
- Because these voters are often concentrated in urban areas, they can affect the outcome of constituency elections significantly. In the early 1990s, _Coxall & Robins_ estimated that there were 49 seats where the margin of victory was smaller than the number of ethnic minority voters in the constituency: so ethnic minority voters had the power to swing the election.
- Black and ethnic minority voters have often supported Labour, possibly because of Labour’s assistance for the deprived urban communities where many minority groups have traditionally lived, and possibly because of the tough Conservative stance on immigration and race relations. In 1997, 89% of Black and 81% of Asians voted Labour (Saggar, 2000). In 2001, Labour had a 67% lead over the Conservatives among Black voters, and a 58% lead amongst Asian supporters. In 2005, however, some Muslim Asians defected to the Liberal Democrats because of the Iraq War. Famously, Labour candidate for Bethnal Green and Bow, Oona King MP, lost her seat to Respect candidate George Galloway over this.
- This left-lean of BME voters also has roots in Enoch Powell’s (Cons) _Rivers of Blood_ anti-immigration speech in 1968, criticising Labour’s more open immigration policy, and Norman Tebbit’s (Cons) 1990 ‘Cricket test’, where he criticised South Asian and Caribbean voters for their lack of loyalty to the England cricket team. This might explain why in 2017, the Conservatives won just 5 of the UK’s 75 most ethnically diverse constituencies, whereas as Labour won 46 of the 73 constituencies in London, which is 4% white British. In 2019, Labour retained a 44% lead over the Conservatives in their share of the national ethnic minority vote.

*****************************************************************************************************

Education:

- This changed significantly in 2017: previously, those with higher educational qualifications were more likely to vote Conservative, perhaps due to the link to higher incomes, whereas those with lower qualifications would vote Labour, perhaps due to the link to socio-economic deprivation. **However, the 2017 results show this has changed:** those with degrees or higher were15% more likely to vote Labour than Conservative, whilst those with no qualifications were 17% more likely to vote Conservative than Labour. Similar patterns emerged in 2019.
- This is perhaps because white working-class voters are most likely to feel threatened by globalisation and immigration, hence their support for Brexit. In his book _The Road to Somewhere_ (2017), David Goodhart argued those with higher qualifications are better positioned to take advantage of globalisation. Labour’s policies have been more liberal on immigration and more nuanced on Brexit.


